<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pekahiah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 742–740 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> · Also known as Pekahiah of Israel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pekahiah was king of Israel for about two years around 742–740 BCE, succeeding his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menahem">Menahem</a>. According to the Books of Kings he was assassinated in his palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a> by his officer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pekah">Pekah</a>, who took the throne and then turned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> in alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pekahiah.jpg" alt="Pekahiah" width="320" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Guillaume Rouille · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pekahiah.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
